There are two conversions we need to do: dollars to cents, and hours to minutes. Let's look at dollars to cents.

Since $1\,\text{dollar}=100\,\text{cents}$, we know that both $\dfrac{1\,\text{dollar}}{100\,\text{cents}}$ and $\dfrac{100\,\text{cents}}{1\,\text{dollar}}$ equal $1$.

So we can multiply by either of those fractions to help us convert units, since multiplying by $1$ doesn't change value. In this problem, we multiply by $\blueD{\dfrac{100\,\text{cents}}{1\,\text{dollar}}}$ since the rate we want to convert uses $\text{dollars}$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}15\,\dfrac{\cancel{\text{dollars}}}{\text{hour}}\cdot \blueD{\dfrac{100\,\text{cents}}{1\,\cancel{\text{dollar}}}}
\\\\
&=1500\,\dfrac{\text{cents}}{\text{hour}}
\end{align}$
